 -0.78}, "rating_margin": -15.3, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.72, "baseline_margin": -10.5, "analyst_adjustment": -0.5, "key_factors": ["District's R+8 partisan lean favors incumbent Andy Harris", "Unanimous Safe/Solid R expert ratings across ten raters", "Harris holds a large fundraising edge ($1.85M cash vs. $73K for Schwartz)", "Possible Maryland redistricting is the main upside risk for Democrats"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Harris: an R+8 seat, incumbency, a big money advantage, a single July poll showing him up 13, and markets pricing him near 90%. All ten expert ratings call the race Safe or Solid R, so only a modest shift toward the challenger is warranted given thin, dated polling.", "watch": "A court-ordered or legislatively enacted redrawing of Maryland's map could put this seat in play and would be the biggest reason to revisit the forecast.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 64 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Andy Harris (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Andy Harris seeks another term in Maryland's Republican-leaning Eastern Shore district, facing Democrat Dan Schwartz. Harris won renomination easily and has out-raised Schwartz by more than three to one, with a cash advantage of roughly $1.8 million to $73,000. Outside spending on both sides has been negligible.\n\nThe evidence points one direction: the district leans Republican by eight points, a July live-caller poll showed Harris ahead 52-39, prediction markets price his chances near 90%, and every major race rater labels the contest Safe or Solid Republican. Headlines about redistricting and critical opinion columns add some noise, but nothing yet suggests a competitive race. Schwartz would need a major external shock to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1047, "margin": -9.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0918, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0624, "margin": -11.0}, {"date":